The best approach for implementing a spell checker feature in a Java application that connects to an SQL database for storing and retrieving word dictionaries would be to create a Java class that connects to the SQL database, retrieves the word dictionaries, and uses them to check the spelling of a given text. Here is a sample code that demonstrates how to check spelling errors in a given text and suggest corrections for any misspelled words using data from an SQL database:
```
import java.sql.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
public class SpellChecker {

    private Connection conn;

    public SpellChecker() throws SQLException {
        // Connect to the SQL database
        conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/dictionary", "username", "password");
    }

    public List<String> checkSpelling(String text) throws SQLException {
        List<String> errors = new ArrayList<>();
        String[] words = text.split("\\s+");
        for (String word : words) {
            if (!isWordInDictionary(word)) {
                errors.add(word);
            }
        }
        return errors;
    }

    public List<String> suggestCorrections(String word) throws SQLException {
        List<String> suggestions = new ArrayList<>();
        // Query the SQL database for words that are similar to the misspelled word
        String query = "SELECT word FROM words WHERE word LIKE '" + word.substring(0, 1) + "%'";
        Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
        ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query);
        while (rs.next()) {
            String suggestion = rs.getString("word");
            if (isSimilar(word, suggestion)) {
                suggestions.add(suggestion);
            }
        }
        return suggestions;
    }

    private boolean isWordInDictionary(String word) throws SQLException {
        // Query the SQL database to check if the word is in the dictionary
        String query = "SELECT COUNT(*) AS count FROM words WHERE word = '" + word + "'";
        Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
        ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query);
        rs.next();
        int count = rs.getInt("count");
        return count > 0;
    }

    private boolean isSimilar(String word1, String word2) {
        // Check if two words are similar by comparing their lengths and characters
        if (Math.abs(word1.length() - word2.length()) > 1) {
            return false;
        }
        int differences = 0;
        for (int i = 0, j = 0; i < word1.length() && j < word2.length(); i++, j++) {
            if (word1.charAt(i) != word2.charAt(j)) {
                differences++;
                if (differences > 1) {
                    return false;
                }
                if (word1.length() > word2.length()) {
                    j--;
                } else if (word1.length() < word2.length()) {
                    i--;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws SQLException {
        SpellChecker spellChecker = new SpellChecker();
        String text = "Ths is a sample text with misspelled wrds.";
        List<String> errors = spellChecker.checkSpelling(text);
        for (String error : errors) {
            List<String> suggestions = spellChecker.suggestCorrections(error);
            System.out.println("Error: " + error + ", Suggestions: " + suggestions);
        }
    }

}
```
In this sample code, the `SpellChecker` class connects to an SQL database named `dictionary` using the JDBC driver for MySQL. The `checkSpelling` method takes a string of text as input, splits it into individual words, and checks if each word is in the dictionary by querying the SQL database. If a word is not in the dictionary, it is added to a list of errors. The `suggestCorrections` method takes a misspelled word as input, queries the SQL database for words that are similar to it, and returns a list of suggestions. The `isWordInDictionary` method and `isSimilar` method are helper methods that check if a word is in the dictionary and if two words are similar, respectively.
To use this sample code, you would need to replace the SQL database URL, username, and password with your own values, and create a table named `words` in the database with a column named `word` that contains all the words in the dictionary. You can then create an instance of the `SpellChecker` class and call its `checkSpelling` method with a string of text to check for spelling errors, and its `suggestCorrections` method with a misspelled word to suggest corrections.

