Here's a sample PHP code that can be used to determine if an IP address is static or dynamic:
function isStaticIP($ip) {
    exec("ipconfig /all", $output);
    foreach ($output as $line) {
        if (strpos($line, "DHCP Enabled") !== false) {
            $dhcp = trim(str_replace("DHCP Enabled", "", $line));
        }
        if (strpos($line, "IP Address") !== false) {
            $temp = explode(":", $line);
            $address = trim($temp[1]);
            if ($address == $ip) {
                if ($dhcp == "Yes") {
                    return false;
                } else {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return false;
}
In a business setting, a static IP address might be appropriate for a server that needs to be accessed by remote employees or clients, as it provides a fixed and reliable address for them to connect to. On the other hand, a dynamic IP address might be more suitable for devices that are constantly on the move, such as laptops or mobile devices, as it allows them to connect to different networks without the need for reconfiguration.

