The 22nd Amendment to the United States Constitution was ratified in 1951 and limits the number of times an individual can serve as President of the United States to two terms. The amendment was introduced in response to President Franklin D. Roosevelt's four-term presidency, which some felt was a violation of the traditional two-term limit set by George Washington. The amendment was intended to prevent any one individual from becoming too powerful and to ensure a peaceful transfer of power in the United States.
To access information on the 22nd Amendment from the United States Constitution using Go code, you can use the Go programming language's built-in HTTP library to make a request to a website that displays the text of the Constitution, such as the website of the National Archives. You can then use a parsing library like GoQuery to extract the text of the 22nd Amendment from the HTML of the website. Here is an example of how to do this:
```go
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"net/http"
	"strings"
	"github.com/PuerkitoBio/goquery"
)
func main() {
	// Make a request to the National Archives website to get the text of the Constitution
	resp, err := http.Get("https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/constitution-transcript")
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()
	// Parse the HTML of the website using GoQuery
	doc, err := goquery.NewDocumentFromReader(resp.Body)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	// Find the text of the 22nd Amendment in the HTML
	var amendmentText string
	doc.Find("#page-container .page-body .row:nth-child(2) .col-md-9 p").Each(func(i int, s *goquery.Selection) {
		if strings.Contains(s.Text(), "22nd Amendment") {
			amendmentText = s.Text()
		}
	})
	// Print the text of the 22nd Amendment
	fmt.Println(amendmentText)
}
```
This code uses the `http.Get` function to make a request to the National Archives website, then uses GoQuery to find the text of the 22nd Amendment in the HTML of the website. The text is then printed to the console. Note that the code assumes that the text of the 22nd Amendment is contained in a `p` element within a `div` element with the class `col-md-9`, which may not be true in all cases. You may need to adjust the code to match the specific HTML structure of the website you are using.

