To modify the code to allow for more than two players, we need to make the following changes:
1. Instead of using a fixed-size array for the scores of two players, we need to use a dynamic array with a size equal to the number of players.
2. We need to add a variable to keep track of the number of players and prompt the user to input the number of players.
3. We need to modify the loop condition to check if any player has a score of 20 or more.
4. We need to modify the player variable to cycle through all players in a round-robin fashion.
Here's the modified code:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    srand(time(NULL));
    int num_players = 0;
    cout << "Enter the number of players: ";
    cin >> num_players;
    int* scores = new int[num_players];
    for (int i = 0; i < num_players; i++)
    {
        scores[i] = 0;
    }
    int player = 0;
    int roll = 0;
    while (true)
    {
        roll = rand() % 6 + 1;
        if (roll == 1)
        {
            scores[player] = 0;
            player = (player + 1) % num_players;
        }
        else
        {
            scores[player] += roll;
        }
        cout << "Player " << player + 1 << " scored " << roll << " and now has " << scores[player] << " points." << endl;
        if (scores[player] >= 20)
        {
            cout << "Player " << player + 1 << " wins with a score of " << scores[player] << "!" << endl;
            break;
        }
        player = (player + 1) % num_players;
    }
    delete[] scores;
    return 0;
}
```
In this modified code, we use a dynamic array `scores` to store the scores of all players. We initialize all scores to 0 in a loop. We also prompt the user to input the number of players and store it in `num_players`.
In the while loop, we first roll the dice and update the score of the current player accordingly. If the current player rolls a 1, we reset their score to 0 and move on to the next player. Otherwise, we add the roll value to the current player's score.
We then print out the score of the current player and check if their score is 20 or more. If it is, we print out the winner and break out of the loop. Otherwise, we move on to the next player.
We use the modulo operator to cycle through all players in a round-robin fashion. Finally, we delete the dynamic array `scores` to free up memory.

